        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.099,
            0.484,
            0.159
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "organic evolution the principle set forth by Darwin that every plant or animal has evolved, or changed, over a long period of time from earlier, simpler forms of life to more complex forms (p. 608)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.157,
            0.482,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "to more people:\northodoxy traditional beliefs, especially in religion (p. 469)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.177,
            0.484,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ostracism in ancient Athens, the process for temporarily banning ambitious politicians from the city by popular vote (p. 123)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.22,
            0.486,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ozone layer a thin layer of gas in the upper atmosphere that shields Earth from the sun's ultraviolet rays (p. 970)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.291,
            0.279,
            0.312,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "P"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.307,
            0.492,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Paleolithic Age from the Greek for \"Old Stone,\" the early period of human history, from approximately 2,500,000 to 10,000 B.C., during which humans used simple stone tools; sometimes called the Old Stone Age (p. 22)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.381,
            0.493,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pan-Africanism the unity of all black Africans, regardless of national boundaries (pp. 788, 923)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.411,
            0.494,
            0.443
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pan-Arabism Arab unity, regardless of national boundaries (p. 931)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.441,
            0.457,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "partisan a resistance fighter in World War II (p. 822)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.455,
            0.497,
            0.502
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "pasha an appointed official in the Ottoman Empire who collected taxes, maintained law and order, and was directly responsible to the sultan's court (p. 459)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.5,
            0.5,
            0.56
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "pastoral nomad a person who domesticates animals for food and clothing and moves along regular migratory routes to provide a steady source of nourishment for those animals (p. 55)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.558,
            0.502,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "paterfamilias in the Roman social structure, the dominant male head of the household, which also included his wife, sons and their wives and children, unmarried daughters, and slaves (p. 165)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.614,
            0.503,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "patriarch the head of the Eastern Orthodox Church, originally appointed by the Byzantine emperor (p. 305)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.648,
            0.372,
            0.663
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "patriarchal dominated by men (p. 41)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.661,
            0.505,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "patrician great landowners, they formed the ruling class in the Roman Republic (p. 152)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.692,
            0.471,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "patrilineal tracing lineage through the father (p. 237)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.709,
            0.507,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "peacekeeping force a military force drawn from neutral members of the United Nations to settle conflicts and supervise truces (p. 975)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.752,
            0.51,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "peninsular a person born on the Iberian Peninsula; typically, a Spanish or Portuguese official who resided temporarily in Latin America for political and economic gain and then returned to Europe (p. 672)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.813,
            0.327,
            0.829
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "per capita per person (p. 943)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.827,
            0.513,
            0.857
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "perestroika Mikhail Gorbachev's plan to reform the Soviet Union by restructuring its economy (p. 877)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.855,
            0.515,
            0.916
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "permanent revolution an atmosphere of constant revolutionary fervor favored by Mao Zedong to enable China to overcome the past and achieve the final stage of communism (p. 942)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.507,
            0.089,
            0.88,
            0.122
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "phalanx a wall of shields created by foot soldiers marching close together in a rectangular formation (p. 116)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.507,
            0.121,
            0.881,
            0.166
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "pharaoh the most common of the various titles for ancient Egyptian monarchs; the term originally meant \"great house\" or \"palace\" (p. 47)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.509,
            0.164,
            0.882,
            0.209
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "philosophe French for \"philosopher\"; applied to all intellectuals—i.e., writers, journalists, economists, and social reformers—during the Enlightenment (p. 519)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.51,
            0.207,
            0.884,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "philosophy an organized system of thought, from the Greek for \"love of wisdom\" (p. 130)"
